<!-- ═════════ PRIVACY (proxy + security, merged) ═════════ -->
<div class="v page" id="v-px">
  <div class="page-h">
    <h1>Privacy &amp; Proxy</h1>
    <p>See what happens between you and a website, and control what gets shared. Everything here runs inside this page, so it only affects the tools on this page, not the rest of your browser.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs" id="ptabs" role="tablist">
    <button data-pt="try" class="on"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>Try the proxy</button>
    <button data-pt="prot"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Protection <span class="badge" id="pbadge" hidden></span></button>
    <button data-pt="act"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-pulse"/></svg>Activity</button>
    <button data-pt="learn"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-book"/></svg>Learn</button>
  </div>

  <!-- Tab 1: try it -->
  <section class="pt-pane on" id="pt-try">
    <div class="usecards">
      <div class="uc good"><b>✓ What this is good for</b>
        <ul>
          <li>Reading a page as plain text, with no scripts, ads or trackers</li>
          <li>Seeing exactly how a web request works, step by step</li>
          <li>Blocking known ad and tracking sites</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="uc bad"><b>✕ What it can't do</b>
        <ul>
          <li>Hide your IP address (sites still see you)</li>
          <li>Get around school or work filters</li>
          <li>Encrypt your traffic (HTTPS does that)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">Make a request</div><p class="pd">A proxy is a middleman: your request goes through a program that can check it, remember the answer and keep a record. Pick a mode, type something, press Go.</p></div></header>
      <div class="px-modes" id="pxm"></div>
      <form class="px-bar" id="pxf" autocomplete="off">
        <input class="inp" id="pxq" type="text" placeholder="Search a topic…" aria-label="Proxy request">
        <button class="btn pri" type="submit">Go</button>
      </form>
      <div class="px-ex" id="pxex"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel px-res" id="pxout"><span class="meta">Results appear here. Not sure where to start? Press one of the examples above.</span></div>

    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-list"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">What the proxy did</div><p class="pd">Every request passes through these six steps. Below you can see what each step decided for your latest request.</p></div></header>
      <div class="px-flow">
        <div class="px-node">You<small>type a search or URL</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">1 Normalize<small>tidy the URL</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">2 Rules<small>allow or block</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">3 Cache<small>seen it before?</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">4 Fetch<small>ask the site</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">5 Transform<small>clean the reply</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node core">6 Log<small>write it down</small></div><div class="px-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="px-node">You<small>see the result</small></div>
      </div>
      <div id="pxtr"><span class="meta">Nothing yet. Run a request above and each step will be listed here.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Tab 2: protection -->
  <section class="pt-pane" id="pt-prot">
    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">Security check-up</div><p class="pd">Live tests of this page's protections, run right now. Amber items are worth fixing; blue items are just information.</p></div></header>
      <div class="sec-top"><div class="ring" id="ring"><b id="scs">–</b></div><div><div class="sec-verdict" id="sverd">Checking…</div><span class="meta" id="scl">checks passed</span></div></div>
      <div id="sck"></div>
      <div class="btns"><button class="btn" id="scr" type="button">Run checks again</button></div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-cookie"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">Block trackers &amp; cookies</div><p class="pd">Third-party cookies are set by a company other than the site you're on (usually advertisers) so they can recognise you across sites. A web page can't change your browser's cookie settings, so Phoenix protects you in three layers:</p></div></header>
      <div class="sec-layers">
        <div class="sec-layer"><em>Always on</em><b>Layer 1: Proxy requests carry no cookies</b><span>Fetch and Search requests are sent with credentials omitted, so no cookies go out and any a site tries to set are ignored. Reader view also removes scripts and images that could set them.</span></div>
        <div class="sec-layer"><em>You choose</em><b>Layer 2: Frame isolation</b><span>Sites shown in Frame mode are boxed in. <b style="display:inline">Strict</b> stops them using their own cookies and storage (and, in Chromium browsers, gives them an empty temporary cookie jar). Some sites that need to remember you won't work in Strict.</span></div>
        <div class="sec-layer"><em>Your browser</em><b>Layer 3: Browser setting</b><span>The only switch that blocks third-party cookies everywhere else. Look in your browser's Privacy or Cookies settings for "third-party cookies" or "cross-site tracking". Safari and Firefox already restrict them by default.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">Layer 2 setting: frame isolation</div>
      <div class="opts" id="sci" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr"></div>
      <div class="btns"><button class="btn" id="sct" type="button">Add common tracker sites to the blocklist</button><span class="meta" id="sctn"></span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-list"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">Site rules</div><p class="pd">Rules run before anything is requested. A blocked site is never contacted. Use these to decide which websites the proxy may reach.</p></div></header>
      <div class="fld"><label for="pxbl">Blocklist: sites to never contact</label>
        <textarea class="inp" id="pxbl" spellcheck="false" placeholder="One site per line, e.g. example.com"></textarea>
        <span class="meta">Subdomains are included: <code>example.com</code> also covers <code>www.example.com</code>.</span></div>
      <div class="opts" style="margin-top:14px" id="pxro"></div>
      <div id="pxalw" style="display:none;margin-top:14px"><div class="fld"><label for="pxal">Allowlist: the only sites the proxy may reach</label>
        <textarea class="inp" id="pxal" spellcheck="false"></textarea></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <header class="ph"><span class="pico"><svg class="i"><use href="#i-lock"/></svg></span><div><div class="pt">PIN lock</div><p class="pd" id="scpin"></p></div></header>
      <div class="btns" style="margin-top:0"><button class="btn" type="button" data-go-set="1">Open lock settings</button></div>
    </div>

    <details class="acc">
      <summary>Technical details: cookies, storage and Content Security Policy</summary>
      <div class="body">
        <div class="sub">Cookie &amp; storage audit</div>
        <p class="px-help">What this page itself has stored in your browser right now (values are never shown).</p>
        <div id="sca"></div>
        <div class="sub">Content Security Policy</div>
        <p class="px-help">A rule list the browser enforces on this page. Even if an attacker injected something, the browser would refuse anything the policy doesn't allow.</p>
        <table class="sec-tbl" id="scp"></table>
        <p class="px-help" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Why <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>?</b> Phoenix is a single file with its code inside it, so inline scripts must be allowed. That is the one weak spot of a one-file design: the policy stops outside resources but can't stop code injected inline. This is why Phoenix also escapes all text it displays and never uses <code>eval</code>.</p>
        <div class="sub">Blocked by the policy this session</div>
        <div class="px-log" id="scv"></div>
      </div>
    </details>
