  <!-- Tab 4: learn -->
  <section class="pt-pane" id="pt-learn">
    <details class="acc" open>
      <summary>The three modes, compared</summary>
      <div class="body px-doc">
        <table>
          <tr><th></th><th>Search</th><th>Fetch URL</th><th>Frame</th></tr>
          <tr><td><b>What it does</b></td><td>Asks Wikipedia's open search API and shows results</td><td>Requests a page itself, then shows a cleaned "reader view"</td><td>Shows the site live inside a sandboxed window</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Real proxy?</b></td><td>Yes, full pipeline</td><td>Yes, full pipeline</td><td>Partly: rules and logging apply, but the browser loads the site itself</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Cache and clean-up</b></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (the proxy never sees the content)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Works on</b></td><td>Wikipedia</td><td>Sites that allow other pages to read them (many APIs, some sites)</td><td>Sites that allow being embedded</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="acc">
      <summary>Why it can't open every site</summary>
      <div class="body px-doc">
        <p>Real proxies run on a server, which isn't limited by browser security rules. This one runs inside your browser, so it obeys them. <strong>CORS</strong> (cross-origin resource sharing) lets a site decide whether other web pages may read its responses; most sites say no. When that happens the trace shows exactly where it failed, and you can switch to Frame mode. Many sites also refuse to be framed (<code>X-Frame-Options</code> or a <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> header), and the browser then shows a blank or error frame. This page can't detect that, so Frame mode has an "open in new tab" button.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="acc">
      <summary>What it does, and does not, do for you</summary>
      <div class="body px-doc">
        <h4>It does not…</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hide your IP address.</strong> The request still comes from your browser, so the site sees you.</li>
          <li><strong>Get around network filters or firewalls.</strong> Your connection is unchanged.</li>
          <li><strong>Add encryption.</strong> Security comes from HTTPS, which the "upgrade http to https" rule helps enforce.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>It does protect you by…</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Blocking hosts you list before any connection is made.</li>
          <li>Removing tracking parameters such as <code>utm_*</code>, <code>fbclid</code> and <code>gclid</code> from URLs.</li>
          <li>Sending no cookies and no referrer, and stripping scripts, frames, stylesheets and images from fetched pages, so the reader view can't run code or phone home.</li>
          <li>Showing Frame content in a sandbox, separated from this page.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="acc">
      <summary>How Phoenix protects this page, and its limits</summary>
      <div class="body px-doc">
        <h4>Hardening in this file</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Content Security Policy:</strong> no external scripts, styles, images or fonts; HTTPS-only connections; no plugins, no form posts, no workers.</li>
          <li><strong>No referrer:</strong> sites you open or embed aren't told which page sent you.</li>
          <li><strong>Output escaping:</strong> text from searches, URLs, settings and backups is inserted as plain text, never as code.</li>
          <li><strong>Backup import filtering:</strong> a backup file can only change known settings of the right type, and can never overwrite the PIN.</li>
          <li><strong>Frame sandbox, permission denial and <code>noopener</code>:</strong> embedded and opened sites can't reach back into this page, and camera, microphone, location and similar permissions are denied to frames.</li>
          <li><strong>PIN storage:</strong> a salted PBKDF2 hash (150,000 rounds of SHA-256), with a lockout after 5 wrong attempts.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Limits you should know about</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Server headers can't be set from a file.</strong> Headers such as <code>X-Frame-Options</code>, <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> (HSTS) and <code>Set-Cookie</code> flags are sent by a web server. A real deployment would add them; here the CSP meta tag covers what a page can do alone.</li>
          <li><strong>The PIN is a screen lock, not encryption.</strong> Anyone with access to your browser's developer tools can read or bypass it, and settings are stored unencrypted in local storage.</li>
          <li><strong>Links opened in a new tab are normal tabs.</strong> They follow your browser's own cookie rules (Layer 3).</li>
          <li><strong>Frame content is the site's own.</strong> Strict mode limits what it can do, but a framed site still sees your IP address.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="acc">
      <summary>Glossary</summary>
      <div class="body px-doc">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cache hit / miss:</strong> an answer was / wasn't already stored.</li>
          <li><strong>Reader view:</strong> just the text and links of a page, without scripts or styling.</li>
          <li><strong>Sandbox:</strong> a browser feature that limits what an embedded page may do.</li>
          <li><strong>Forward vs reverse proxy:</strong> a forward proxy works for the user (this tool); a reverse proxy sits in front of a website and works for the site (load balancing, caching).</li>
          <li><strong>First-party / third-party cookie:</strong> set by the site you're on / by another domain embedded in it.</li>
          <li><strong>Credentialless frame:</strong> a frame loaded without your existing cookies or storage.</li>
          <li><strong>PBKDF2 / salt:</strong> a deliberately slow hash method, and a random value that makes every stored hash unique.</li>
          <li><strong>XSS:</strong> cross-site scripting, where an attacker gets their code to run inside a page.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</div>

